//! lotus/expand_env_hold.rye — ALES62: an attack/HOLD/release downward expander — the breathing expander given the one stage
//! between its two knobs, and the rung that completes the HELD dynamics quartet. ALES61 gave the expander (ALES60) a patient sense
//! of time, driving the deepened floor from ALES52's proven envelope follower so it eases in as a sound arrives and eases out as it
//! falls away. Yet an un-held expander still deepens and re-opens on a brief DIP between two loud samples — it pumps, and the ear
//! hears the gain move. A real expander carries a HOLD knob for exactly this: on a fall away from a peak, it pins the peak for a
//! little while BEFORE the release begins, so the widening stays idle through the dip and the passage stays whole. ALES56 built that
//! hold stage INSIDE the follower (hold_env.env_step_hold) and proved it once; ALES57–59 spent it for the gate, compressor, and
//! limiter. This rung spends the same proof for the expander — the fourth and last member — so the held quartet mirrors the un-held
//! quartet exactly. The gain math is not re-invented (it is ALES60's widening), and the time base is not re-invented (it is ALES56's
//! held env_step); this rung is exactly their composition over public APIs, adding only the hold knob.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES62.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-184011_fill-ales62-lotus-attack-hold-release-expander.md
//! Stands on: lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, sample_min/sample_max, max_clip, the range law shape; lotus/expand.rye (ALES60)
//! — the deepened floor this drives over time, re-proven beside it; lotus/hold_env.rye (ALES56) — the HELD time base, its
//! env_step_hold advancing the follower with the hold pinning the peak; lotus/expand_env.rye (ALES61) — the un-held attack/release
//! expander this reduces to at a hold of zero, re-proven beside it as the zero-hold limit.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Bounded in-process i16 PCM buffers on one bench, siloed to lotus/. No socket,
//! no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real sample rate — the attack, release, and hold are counted in sample INDICES,
//! not milliseconds against a clock, and the threshold is a magnitude in sample units, the ratio a plain fraction. One held envelope
//! step and at most one widen and one multiply-divide per sample; nothing on the path can overflow, since the expanded envelope is
//! at most the envelope so the gain sits in [0, 1] and every written magnitude is at most the input's, which already fits i16.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/expand_env_hold.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const envelope = @import("envelope.rye");
const hold_env = @import("hold_env.rye");
const expand_env = @import("expand_env.rye");

pub const expand_env_hold_version = "20260814.184011";

/// The attack/hold/release expander's faults, one consistent name each: a threshold outside [1, sample_max], a ratio below unity or
/// with a zero denominator, an attack or release coefficient outside (0, 1], a hold longer than the widest clip, and a span outside
/// the samples. Each coincides by name with the tool it borrows from — BadThreshold and BadRatio from the expander, BadCoeff from
/// the follower, BadHold from the held follower, BadRange from the whole family — so a composer sees one consistent fault surface.
pub const ExpandEnvHoldError = error{
    /// A threshold of zero (no band left below it to expand) or above sample_max (everything would fall below it) — refused.
    BadThreshold,
    /// A ratio below unity (would narrow the deficit, not widen it) or with a zero denominator — refused, no write.
    BadRatio,
    /// An attack or release coefficient outside (0, 1] — a frozen envelope, an overshooting step, or a divide by zero.
    BadCoeff,
    /// A hold longer than max_clip samples — no clip is that long, so such a hold could never expire within a span; refused, no write.
    BadHold,
    /// A span outside the current samples — refused, no write.
    BadRange,
};

/// Expand `count` samples starting at `start` in place below the magnitude `threshold` by the ratio `ratio_num/ratio_den`, the
/// downward widening FOLLOWING a HELD envelope that CONTINUES from the caller's `env` and `hold_left` rather than from silence — the
/// carried-state form (ALES43's lesson), now carrying BOTH the envelope and the hold countdown so the hold survives across a call
/// boundary. Each sample advances the proven ALES56 held follower one step (attack while rising and rearming the hold, the peak
/// pinned while the countdown lasts, the release only once it expires); when the held envelope sits at or above the threshold the
/// expander is idle and the sample passes byte-for-byte, and when it falls below, the envelope's deficit is widened by the ratio
/// (ALES60's deepened floor) and that gain applied to the raw sample. With `hold_samples` zero the held follower reduces to ALES52's
/// plain env_step, so this expander reduces to ALES61's un-held expander byte-for-byte. Refuses BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff,
/// BadHold, and BadRange before any write; on refusal the clip and the carried state are untouched. Values only — never the length.
pub fn expand_hold_follow_carry(
    clip: *timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    env: *i64,
    hold_left: *u32,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) ExpandEnvHoldError!void {
    // invariant: the threshold is a legal magnitude — at least one sample unit and no greater than the i16 rail. Before any write.
    if (threshold < 1) return error.BadThreshold;
    if (threshold > @as(i32, timeline.sample_max)) return error.BadThreshold;
    // invariant: the ratio is at least one (num >= den) and its denominator is nonzero — a ratio below one would narrow the deficit.
    if (ratio_den == 0) return error.BadRatio;
    if (ratio_num < ratio_den) return error.BadRatio;
    // invariant: both attack and release coefficients are legal fractions in (0, 1] — checked by the follower's one proven rule.
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(attack_num, attack_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    if (!envelope.coeff_ok(release_num, release_den)) return error.BadCoeff;
    // invariant: the hold is bounded by the widest clip — a longer hold could never expire within any span. Before any write.
    if (hold_samples > timeline.max_clip) return error.BadHold;
    // invariant: the span lies wholly within the current samples — checked without underflow (count vs the remainder).
    if (start > clip.len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > clip.len - start) return error.BadRange;

    const thr: i64 = @as(i64, threshold);
    const num: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_num);
    const den: i64 = @as(i64, ratio_den);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < count) : (i += 1) {
        const x: i16 = clip.buf[start + i];
        // Advance the proven ALES56 HELD follower one sample; e is the smoothed-and-held magnitude, in [0, sample_max].
        _ = hold_env.env_step_hold(env, hold_left, x, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
        const e: i64 = env.*;
        // invariant: the envelope stays a legal magnitude — never negative, never past the rail (the held step's own guarantee).
        assert(e >= 0 and e <= @as(i64, timeline.sample_max));
        if (e < thr) {
            if (e == 0) {
                // The held detector reads exact silence. env_step forces at least a unit step toward any nonzero target and the hold
                // only ever PINS a peak (it never lowers the envelope), so a zero envelope can only arise when this sample's own
                // magnitude is zero — nothing to expand, and no divide by zero.
                // invariant: the tail is fully attenuated when this runs, which is what the
                // envelope exists to reach -- a residue here means the release never completed
                // and the next clip would open over it
                assert(x == 0);
            } else {
                // The expanded envelope target — ALES60's deepened floor, computed on the ENVELOPE not the bare sample: the deficit
                // thr − e widened by the ratio (num/den >= 1) and floored at zero. The widened deficit fits i64 (thr−e <= 32767).
                const deficit: i64 = thr - e; // strictly positive here (e < thr)
                const widened: i64 = @divTrunc(deficit * num, den);
                const raw_exp: i64 = thr - widened;
                const exp: i64 = if (raw_exp < 0) 0 else raw_exp; // floor at silence — the gate reached from within the expander
                // invariant: the expanded envelope sits within [0, envelope] — the gain exp/e is in [0, 1] (num/den >= 1 => exp <= e).
                assert(exp >= 0 and exp <= e);
                const m: i64 = if (x < 0) -@as(i64, x) else @as(i64, x); // |x| — safe in i64 (|sample_min| = 32768 fits)
                // Apply the gain exp/e to the raw sample. m·exp <= 32768·32767 fits i64; divTrunc keeps it at most m.
                const new_m: i64 = @divTrunc(m * exp, e);
                // invariant: the gain reduces, never boosts — a smoothed expander only ever leans down.
                assert(new_m <= m);
                const y: i16 = @intCast(if (x < 0) -new_m else new_m); // safe: 0 <= new_m <= m <= 32768 fits i16 by sign
                clip.buf[start + i] = y;
                // invariant: the written sample holds the input's sign (magnitude only shrank).
                assert((x >= 0 and y >= 0) or (x <= 0 and y <= 0));
            }
        }
        // a sample whose held envelope sits at or above the threshold is left exactly as it was — the expander is idle, the hold
        // keeping it idle through a brief dip that would otherwise have deepened it.
    }
    // invariant: an expander changes sample values only, never the clip's length or bound.
    assert(start + count <= clip.len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

/// Expand `count` samples starting at `start` in place, the downward widening following a HELD envelope that begins FROM SILENCE (a
/// fresh env of 0 with the hold unarmed) — one implementation shared with the carried form, so the two cannot drift. Refuses
/// BadThreshold, BadRatio, BadCoeff, BadHold, and BadRange before any write.
pub fn expand_hold_follow(
    clip: *timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    threshold: i32,
    ratio_num: u32,
    ratio_den: u32,
    attack_num: u32,
    attack_den: u32,
    release_num: u32,
    release_den: u32,
    hold_samples: u32,
) ExpandEnvHoldError!void {
    var env: i64 = 0;
    var hold_left: u32 = 0;
    return expand_hold_follow_carry(clip, start, count, &env, &hold_left, threshold, ratio_num, ratio_den, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den, hold_samples);
}

// --- selftest: silence stays silence; a HOLD OF ZERO reduces to the plain ALES61 attack/release expander byte-for-byte (the
//     zero-hold limit); UNIT RATIO is the identity everywhere; a quiet signal has its floor deepened with the sign held and the
//     magnitude never expanding; a NONZERO HOLD keeps the expander idle across a brief dip (it does not pump, the knob monotone);
//     the state CARRIES — env and hold_left both — so a span in two pieces equals the whole once; and a bad threshold, a sub-unity
//     ratio, an illegal coefficient, a hold past max_clip, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name -----------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

fn abs16(v: i16) i32 {
    return if (v < 0) -@as(i32, v) else @as(i32, v);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. silence stays silence — an all-zero clip expands to all zeros at any legal threshold, ratio, coefficients, and hold.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try expand_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 3);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;
    }

    // 2. A HOLD OF ZERO IS THE PLAIN ALES61 EXPANDER — with hold_samples == 0 the held follower's fall branch never pins, so every
    //    sample moves through ALES52's env_step exactly as ALES61's un-held expander does: the held expander with no hold IS the
    //    ALES61 expander, byte-for-byte by construction. Proven by running BOTH tools on the same signal (the zero-hold limit, the
    //    sibling of the family's unit-coefficient identities).
    {
        const mixed = [_]i16{ 800, -800, 5000, -600, 200, -9000, 32767, -32767, 400 };
        var held = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&held, &mixed);
        try expand_hold_follow(&held, 0, held.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5, 0); // hold of zero, real attack/release
        var plain = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&plain, &mixed);
        try expand_env.expand_follow(&plain, 0, plain.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 3, 1, 5); // the proven ALES61 un-held expander
        if (!eq(held.samples(), plain.samples())) return error.ZeroHoldNotPlainExpand;
    }

    // 3. UNIT RATIO IS THE IDENTITY EVERYWHERE — ratio_num == ratio_den makes the expanded envelope equal the envelope, so the gain
    //    is exactly one at every sample regardless of smoothing or hold: the whole signal passes byte-for-byte, even below threshold.
    {
        const quiet = [_]i16{ 300, -300, 5000, -20, 20, -400 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &quiet);
        try expand_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1000, 3, 3, 1, 4, 1, 8, 4); // unit ratio 3:3, real attack/release, hold 4
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &quiet)) return error.UnitRatioNotIdentity;
    }

    // 4. THE SIGN IS HELD AND THE MAGNITUDE NEVER EXPANDS — a quiet signal expands so every output magnitude is at most the input's
    //    and every sign matches, under a real attack, release, and hold.
    {
        const mixed = [_]i16{ 200, -200, 5000, -300, 150, -9000, 250, -250 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &mixed);
        try expand_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 2, 1, 6, 2);
        const out = clip.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < out.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(out[i]) > abs16(mixed[i])) return error.MagnitudeExpanded; // the gain only ever reduces
            if ((mixed[i] > 0 and out[i] < 0) or (mixed[i] < 0 and out[i] > 0)) return error.SignFlipped;
        }
    }

    // 5. A NONZERO HOLD KEEPS THE EXPANDER IDLE ACROSS A BRIEF DIP — a loud sample, one quiet dip below the threshold, then loud
    //    again. With INSTANT attack and INSTANT release and NO hold, the dip drops the envelope below the threshold and is deepened
    //    (the expander pumped down). With a hold that spans the dip, the peak is pinned above the threshold so the expander stays
    //    idle and the dip passes byte-for-byte. So the held magnitude is no SMALLER than the un-held one at every sample (the hold
    //    knob is monotone), and strictly LOUDER at the dip (the expander audibly held idle rather than pumping).
    {
        const dip = [_]i16{ 15000, 300, 15000 }; // loud, a quiet dip below threshold, loud again
        const thr: i32 = 1000;
        var none = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&none, &dip);
        try expand_hold_follow(&none, 0, none.len, thr, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0); // instant attack, instant release, no hold
        var held = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&held, &dip);
        try expand_hold_follow(&held, 0, held.len, thr, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1); // same, hold of 1 spans the single dip sample
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < dip.len) : (i += 1) {
            if (abs16(held.samples()[i]) < abs16(none.samples()[i])) return error.HoldExpandedMore;
        }
        // the dip sample (index 1): the held expander still reads the pinned peak above threshold and lets it pass, the un-held
        // expander has already dropped below threshold and deepens it — the expander held idle through the dip instead of pumping.
        if (abs16(held.samples()[1]) <= abs16(none.samples()[1])) return error.HoldDidNotSurvive;
    }

    // 6. THE STATE CARRIES — env AND hold_left both — so expanding a span in two pieces, the split falling MID-HOLD, equals expanding
    //    the whole once, byte-for-byte: the seam invisible, so dynamics can span and automate and the hold survives across a boundary.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 12000, 12000, 400, 400, 400, 9000, 200, 200 };
        var whole = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&whole, &sig);
        try expand_hold_follow(&whole, 0, whole.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 6, 2);

        var split = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&split, &sig);
        var env: i64 = 0;
        var hold_left: u32 = 0;
        try expand_hold_follow_carry(&split, 0, 3, &env, &hold_left, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 6, 2); // first piece — split at index 3 mid-hold
        try expand_hold_follow_carry(&split, 3, split.len - 3, &env, &hold_left, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 6, 2); // second continues env AND hold_left
        if (!eq(split.samples(), whole.samples())) return error.CarryNotEqual;
    }

    // 7a. a zero threshold refuses BadThreshold before any write, the clip untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (expand_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 0, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.ZeroThresholdSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongZeroThreshold;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedThresholdMutated;
    }

    // 7b. a sub-unity ratio (would narrow the deficit) refuses BadRatio before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (expand_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1000, 1, 2, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.SubUnitySlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRatio) return error.WrongSubUnity;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRatioMutated;
    }

    // 7c. an illegal release coefficient (numerator past the denominator) refuses BadCoeff before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (expand_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 3, 2, 2)) |_| return error.BadReleaseSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongBadRelease;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedCoeffMutated;
    }

    // 7d. a hold longer than max_clip (no clip is that long) refuses BadHold before any write.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (expand_hold_follow(&clip, 0, clip.len, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, timeline.max_clip + 1)) |_| return error.BadHoldSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadHold) return error.WrongBadHold;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedHoldMutated;
    }

    // 7e. an out-of-range span refuses BadRange before any write, leaving the clip untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (expand_hold_follow(&clip, 2, 5, 1000, 4, 1, 1, 4, 1, 8, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedRangeMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-expand-env-hold: an attack/HOLD/release downward expander drives ALES60's deepened floor from ALES56's HELD " ++
        "envelope — each sample advances the held follower one step (attack rising and rearming the hold, the peak pinned while the " ++
        "countdown lasts, the release only once it expires), and when the held envelope falls below the threshold its deficit is " ++
        "widened by the ratio and that gain applied to the raw sample. Silence stays silence; a HOLD OF ZERO reduces to the plain " ++
        "ALES61 attack/release expander BYTE-FOR-BYTE (the zero-hold limit, proven by running both tools); UNIT RATIO is the " ++
        "identity everywhere; a quiet signal has its floor deepened with the sign held and never expanding; a NONZERO HOLD keeps " ++
        "the expander idle across a brief dip (it does not pump, the knob monotone); the state CARRIES — env and hold_left both — " ++
        "so a span in two pieces equals the whole once (the seam invisible, the hold surviving the boundary); and a zero threshold " ++
        "refuses BadThreshold, a sub-unity ratio BadRatio, an illegal coefficient BadCoeff, a hold past max_clip BadHold, an " ++
        "out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched; the HELD dynamics quartet is now whole — gate, compressor, " ++
        "limiter, expander all reading ALES56's one proven held follower; no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, " ++
        "no real sample rate\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
